Q: Concatenating Stringified Strings Doesn&#39;t Truly Concatenate [C++ pre-processor] A: <p>In C++ we tend NOT to use the preprocessor if it can be avoided.<br />
And  I would use some constexpr type safe functionality to do this.<br />
This means that you let the compiler not the preprocessor do the work,<br />
and you will still end up with constant strings in your assembly.</p>
<p>Live demo : <a href="https://onlinegdb.com/oHxQ2setn7" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://onlinegdb.com/oHxQ2setn7</a></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &quot;ce_string.h&quot;

constexpr ce_string str1{&quot;/sys/devices/platform/soc/9c0000.qcom,qupv3_0_geni_se/984000.i2c/i2c-1/&quot;};
constexpr ce_string str2{ &quot;1-002b&quot; };
constexpr ce_string str3{ &quot;2-002f&quot; };

constexpr ce_string addr1 = str1 + str2;
constexpr ce_string addr2 = str1 + str3;

int main()
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; addr1.c_str() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And the header file for the compile time string creation:</p>
<pre><code>#pragma once

#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;utility&gt;

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

template &lt;std::size_t capacity_v&gt;
class ce_string
{
public:
    constexpr ce_string() = default;
    ~ce_string() = default;

    constexpr ce_string(const char (&amp;str)[capacity_v]) : 
        m_size{capacity_v}
    {
        for (std::size_t n{0ul}; n &lt; capacity_v; ++n)
        {
            m_str[n] = str[n];
        }
    }

    constexpr const char* c_str() const noexcept
    {
        return m_str.data();
    }

    constexpr char* str() noexcept
    {
        return m_str.data();
    }

    constexpr std::size_t size() const noexcept
    {
        return m_size;
    }

private:
    std::array&lt;char, capacity_v + 1&gt; m_str{}; // +1 for null terminator
    std::size_t m_size{0ul};
};

template &lt;std::size_t lhs_capacity_v, std::size_t rhs_capacity_v&gt;
constexpr ce_string&lt;lhs_capacity_v + rhs_capacity_v&gt; operator+(const ce_string&lt;lhs_capacity_v&gt;&amp; lhs, const ce_string&lt;rhs_capacity_v&gt;&amp; rhs) noexcept
{
    ce_string&lt;lhs_capacity_v + rhs_capacity_v&gt; result{};

    const char* lhs_str = lhs.c_str();
    const char* rhs_str = rhs.c_str();
    char* result_str = result.str();

    // copy lhs
    for (std::size_t n{0ul}; n &lt; lhs.size(); ++n)
    {
        result_str[n] = lhs_str[n];
    }

    // copy rhs
    for (std::size_t n{0ul}; n &lt; rhs.size(); ++n)
    {
        result_str[lhs.size() + n] = rhs_str[n];
    }

    return result;
}

// add a decuction guide so we deduce capacity 
// from the input string's call to the constructor
template &lt;std::size_t size&gt;
ce_string(const char (&amp;)[size]) -&gt; ce_string&lt;size&gt;;
</code></pre>

Q: SQL query select from all tables where ID equals A: <p>Well you can always use a union:</p>

<pre><code>SELECT * FROM Table_name_1
WHERE hContactId = 200;
UNION  
SELECT * FROM Table_name_2 
WHERE hContactId = 200;
UNION  
SELECT * FROM Table_name_3 
WHERE hContactId = 200;
</code></pre>

Q: SAPUI5 Filter Bar Get filter values for each filter item in the filter bar A: <p>There are a few ways to do this.. but with your current code, I would suggest the following:</p>

<p><strong>The short answer to your question:</strong> </p>

<p>The FilterBar has a method <code>determineControlByFilterItem</code> you can use to get the control for the filter item, which you can then use to get the selected value. </p>

<pre><code>var oFilterItem = oEvent.getSource().getFilterItems()[0];
var oControl = oFilterBar.determineControlByFilterItem(oFilterItem);
var sSelectedValue = oControl.getSelectedKey();
</code></pre>

<p>Be cautious, though, when hard coding array indexes like this. For a more complete solution to your problem, I've suggested a full approach below. </p>

<p><strong>The long answer if you want to use the filter bar to filter a result set:</strong></p>

<p>First, make sure the names of your filter items align to the name of the property you want to filter by. So in your case, your filter items are named "A" and "B"... I suggest you change these to match the property name you want to filter by. Assuming that the names of the properties you want to filter by are "User" and "Location":</p>

<pre><code>&lt;FilterItem name="User" label="User"&gt;
...
&lt;FilterItem name="Location" label="Location"&gt;
...
</code></pre>

<p>Then in your controller, you can use those names to build an array of <code>sap.ui.model.Filter</code> objects that you can use to filter your result set.</p>

<pre><code>onSearch: function(oEvent) {
    //get the filter bar from the event
    var oFilterBar = oEvent.getSource();

    //get the filter items from the filter bar
    var aFilterItems = oFilterBar.getFilterItems();

    //map the array of FilterItems to a new array of sap.ui.model.Filter objects
    var aFilters = aFilterItems.map(function(oFilterItem) {
        //get the filter item name (which is now the same as the filter property name)
        var sFilterName = oFilterItem.getName();

        //use the filter bar to get the control for the filter item
        var oControl = oFilterBar.determineControlByFilterItem(oFilterItem);

        //use the control to get the selected value (selected key)
        var sSelectedValue = oControl.getSelectedKey();

        //use the filter item/property name and the selected value to create a new sap.ui.model.Filter
        var oFilter = new sap.ui.model.Filter(sFilterName, "EQ", sSelectedValue);

        //return the Filter object to the new array
        return oFilter
    });

    //use the array of sap.ui.model.Filter objects to filter your table

    //if you're using a responsive table (sap.m.Table), use:
    this.getView().byId("yourTableId").getBinding("items").filter(aFilters);

    //or if you're using a grid table (sap.ui.table.Table), use:
    this.getView().byId("yourTableId").getBinding("rows").filter(aFilters);
}
</code></pre>

<p>The reason I suggest this approach as opposed to others, is because it scales nicely. Say, for example, that you want to add a third <code>Select</code> control to filter by, you simply need to add a new <code>&lt;FilterItem name="NewFilterProperty" label="New Filter Property"&gt;</code>. Because we didn't hard code anything into the event handler, it will still work without any additional changes. </p>

<p>The only thing to lookout for is if you use different types of controls in your FilterBar. So, for example, if you added an <code>&lt;Input&gt;</code> instead of a <code>&lt;Select&gt;</code>, you'll need to adjust the logic of your event handler to handle this. </p>

<p>I usually do something like this: </p>

<pre><code>onSearch: function(oEvent) {
    var oFilterBar = oEvent.getSource();
    var aFilterItems = oFilterBar.getFilterItems();
    var aFilters = aFilterItems.map(function(oFilterItem) {
        var sFilterName = oFilterItem.getName();
        var oControl = oFilterBar.determineControlByFilterItem(oFilterItem);
        var sValue;
        if (oControl.getMetadata().getName() === "sap.m.Select") {
            sValue = oControl.getSelectedKey();
        } else if (oControl.getMetadata().getName() === "sap.m.Input") {
            sValue = oControl.getValue();
        }
        var oFilter = new sap.ui.model.Filter(sFilterName, "EQ", sValue);
        return oFilter;
    });

}
</code></pre>

Q: YouTube video in WebView gives error code 153 on Android A: <p>I started getting this error randomly on a site I'm developing. I grabbed the debug code (right click on the embed) and it spit out this error:</p>
<pre><code>embedder.identity.missing.referrer
</code></pre>
<p>In incognito, it seems to be fine. One solution I found was using a different domain for the embed, which worked, but it did not track views correctly anymore:  <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code> – a privacy-centric alternative.</p>
<p>I thought it was related to what Google account is logged into the same browser instance you're using, but when I dug further I had the same embed in the old version of the site, but it had one difference:</p>
<pre><code>referrerpolicy=&quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>I added this to my element and the video popped back up right away. Hopefully this helps.</p>
<p>It seems to have cropped up in quite a few other places now, including Chromium extensions and even just embedding iFrames using YouTube's <em>own embed code</em>. Looks like potentially some changes to Chromium caused this, which of course becomes widespread.</p>
<p>I scoured a few different threads and came across a number of potential fixes, and the first one below worked instantly. Add this to your header:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta name=&quot;referrer&quot; content=&quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot;&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Here's NGINX, add inside your server { } code:</p>
<pre><code>add_header Referrer-Policy &quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Also Apache, add to your <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/.htaccess" rel="nofollow noreferrer">.htaccess</a> file:</p>
<pre><code>Header set Referrer-Policy &quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot;
</code></pre>
